// ---- GREED ISLAND ARC (3 nodes) ----
HXH_NODES['hxh_greed_island'] = {text:`Greed Island. Not a place — a Nen construct. A video game made real by some of the most powerful Nen users in history, including Ging Freecss himself. You enter through a console purchased at the Yorknew auction, your consciousness pulled into a world where the rules of reality are rewritten by spell cards.\n\nThe island materializes around you: lush forests, impossible architecture, a sky that feels slightly too close. Your Nen works here, but differently — refined, channeled through the game's systems. Every item, every creature, every interaction is governed by card mechanics layered over genuine combat.\n\nGon and Killua are already here, weeks ahead of you. Gon because this game is his father's creation — every secret in Greed Island is a breadcrumb Ging left for his son. Killua because wherever Gon goes, he follows.\n\nThey have found a teacher: Biscuit Krueger, a woman who appears to be a petite blonde girl of twelve but is actually a fifty-seven-year-old master martial artist who trained under Netero. Her true form is a muscular colossus she refuses to reveal because, in her words, "beauty is the ultimate Nen condition."\n\nBisky puts you through hell. Ren endurance drills until your aura bleeds. Ko exercises until your fists glow white-hot. Gyo training where she hides objects in her aura and punishes you for every one you miss.\n\n"Your Hatsu is a blunt instrument," she says, flicking your forehead hard enough to leave a bruise. "Nen rewards elegance. Power without refinement is just noise."\n\nMeanwhile, other players are dying. A group called the Bombers — led by a man named Genthru — has been systematically murdering players and stealing their cards. Genthru's ability, Little Flower, detonates Nen bombs he plants through physical contact. A handshake. A pat on the back. Every friendly gesture a potential death sentence.\n\nThe game has one hundred designated slot cards. Collect them all, and you clear the game — earning a reward Ging designed specifically for whoever proved worthy enough to finish.`,scene:'adventure',mood:'#1a8a1a',characters:['Gon','Killua','Bisky'],effects:{stats:{str:1,int:1},relationships:{gon:1,killua:1}},choices:[{text:'Focus on Bisky\'s training — raw power means nothing without technique',next:'hxh_greed_training',effects:{stats:{str:2,per:1},relationships:{bisky:2}}},{text:'Hunt spell cards aggressively — the Bombers are closing in and you need leverage',next:'hxh_greed_cards',effects:{stats:{int:2,cou:1}}},{text:'Track the Bombers — Genthru has killed too many players already',next:'hxh_greed_cards',effects:{stats:{cou:2,per:1}}}]};
HXH_NODES['hxh_greed_training'] = {text:`Bisky does not train you the way Wing did. Wing taught you Nen like a language — vocabulary, grammar, careful progression. Bisky teaches it like war.\n\nShe wakes you before dawn for sparring sessions where she uses ten percent of her power — which is still enough to hospitalize you. She corrects your Ko distribution mid-combat by striking the exact points where your aura is thin. Every bruise is a lesson. Every broken bone is a paragraph in a textbook written in pain.\n\n"Wing gave you the foundation," she says, watching you gasp on the ground. "I am giving you the architecture. Foundation without architecture is a slab of concrete. Architecture without foundation is a sandcastle. You need both."\n\nGon and Killua train beside you, pushing each other with the natural rivalry of best friends. Gon develops Jajanken — rock-paper-scissors translated into Nen. Rock: a devastating Ko punch that concentrates all his aura into his fist. Paper: an emission blast. Scissors: a transmuted blade. Simple, powerful, perfectly Gon.\n\nKillua refines Godspeed — converting his aura into electricity. His synapses fire at inhuman speed, his reaction time dropping below the threshold of conscious thought. He moves before he decides to move. The assassin's body acting on trained instinct, now amplified by Nen.\n\nYour own Hatsu sharpens. The vow you made during development tightens, and the power it grants deepens in response. Bisky watches the change with professional satisfaction.\n\n"There it is," she says. "That is what mastery looks like. Not more power — less waste."\n\nWeeks pass. Your card collection grows. Bisky teaches you card combinations — spell cards that can steal from other players, defend against attacks, or teleport across the island. The game-within-a-game is as complex as any Nen battle.\n\nThen Genthru makes his move. He announces publicly that he has placed bombs on every player who ever shook his hand. Dozens of players. One condition to defuse: surrender all your cards. Or die.\n\nGon's eyes harden. Not with rage — with calculation. He has a plan. And it is exactly the kind of insane, brilliant, stubborn plan that only Gon Freecss could devise.`,scene:'training',mood:'#ff6600',characters:['Gon','Killua','Bisky'],effects:{stats:{str:1,per:1},relationships:{bisky:2,gon:1}},choices:[{text:'Follow Gon\'s plan to fight Genthru — trust the boy who never backs down',next:'hxh_greed_cards',effects:{stats:{cou:2},relationships:{gon:2}}},{text:'Propose your own strategy — Genthru\'s bombs have a weakness if you can find it',next:'hxh_greed_cards',effects:{stats:{int:2,per:1}}}]};
HXH_NODES['hxh_greed_cards'] = {text:`The plan is beautiful in its insanity. Gon will fight Genthru directly — not to overpower him, but to survive long enough for a trap to close. Killua and you handle the other two Bombers. Bisky watches the perimeter, ready to intervene if anyone dies.\n\nGenthru is strong. Experienced. His Little Flower detonates on contact, and his combat sense is honed by years of killing. But Gon has something Genthru does not: a willingness to sacrifice his own body that borders on clinical madness.\n\nGon lets Genthru grab his hand. The bomb detonates, mangling Gon's fingers. But in that moment of contact, Gon uses his other hand to slap a spell card onto Genthru's chest — Levy, a card that teleports the target into a sealed space. Genthru vanishes.\n\nYour fight is shorter but no less brutal. The Bomber you face relies on trap-based Nen — proximity mines scattered across the terrain. You navigate them using Gyo to spot the aura residue, closing distance step by careful step until your Hatsu connects and the fight ends.\n\nKillua disables his opponent in eleven seconds. Godspeed is not a fair ability. He almost apologizes.\n\nThe Bombers are defeated. Their card collections are seized. Between your group and the cards you have gathered, you have enough to clear the game.\n\nGon stands before the final card — Patch of Shore, the last designated slot. His hands are bandaged, his body battered, but his eyes shine. "Dad made this game for me," he says quietly. "Every challenge, every card, every puzzle — it was all a letter. The longest letter anyone has ever written."\n\nHe completes the game. The reward is a choice of three cards to take into the real world. Gon chooses Accompany — a card that takes you to a person. He names his father.\n\nThe card activates. Light engulfs the group. When it fades, you are standing on a mountaintop, facing not Ging but Kite — one of Ging's students. The card went to the person closest to Ging who was willing to be found.\n\nGing, as always, is one step ahead. And one step away.\n\n"He left a message with me," Kite says, a long-haired man with kind, tired eyes. "He said: 'If you made it this far, you are ready for what comes next.' Then he mentioned something about Chimera Ants in a country called NGL."\n\nKite's expression darkens. "I was heading there anyway. These Ants... they are different. They are evolving. And if they learn Nen, this world will never be the same."`,scene:'adventure',mood:'#d4a017',characters:['Gon','Killua','Bisky','Kite'],effects:{stats:{cou:1,int:1},relationships:{gon:2}},choices:[{text:'Go with Kite and Gon to NGL — the Chimera Ants cannot wait',next:'hxh_ant_briefing',effects:{stats:{cou:2},relationships:{gon:1}}},{text:'Return to Yorknew — the Troupe is regrouping and Kurapika needs allies',next:'hxh_troupe_heist',effects:{stats:{int:1},relationships:{kurapika:1}}},{text:'Seek out more training — Bisky offered to teach you her master\'s techniques',next:'hxh_hatsu_develop',effects:{stats:{str:1,per:1},relationships:{bisky:2}}},{text:'The Black Whale departs. Beyond the known world — the Dark Continent awaits.',next:'hxh_dark_continent',effects:{stats:{cou:2}}}]};
// ---- MERUEM'S HUMANITY ARC (3 nodes) ----
HXH_NODES['hxh_meruem_komugi'] = {text:`You witness something no one expected. Something that rewrites everything you understood about the Chimera Ant threat.\n\nMeruem — the King, the apex predator, the being born to devour and dominate — is sitting across a board game table from a blind girl. And he is losing.\n\nKomugi is the world champion of Gungi, a strategy game of staggering complexity. She is small, sickly, perpetually dripping with a runny nose she never wipes. She has no Nen. No combat ability. No awareness of the apocalypse unfolding around her. She is, by every metric the King values, worthless.\n\nAnd she beats him. Again. And again. And again.\n\nYou watch from the shadows — scouting the palace before the invasion, cataloguing the King's patterns. What you see defies every briefing Netero gave you.\n\nMeruem does not kill Komugi for defeating him. He does not eat her. Instead, he asks her to play again. And his voice, when he speaks to her, lacks the cold contempt he shows everything else. There is something in it you cannot name at first.\n\nThen you recognize it. Curiosity. Genuine, bewildered curiosity — the confusion of an omnipotent being encountering the one thing power cannot provide: understanding why a fragile, blind, insignificant human can do something he cannot.\n\n"Why do you not fear me?" Meruem asks her.\n\n"Should I?" Komugi replies, placing a piece. "You are my opponent. The best opponent I have ever had. Why would I fear the person who makes me play my best?"\n\nMeruem stares at the board. His Royal Guards — Pitou, Pouf, Youpi — watch from the doorway with expressions ranging from confusion to horror. Their King, designed by evolution to consume humanity, is being humanized by a board game.\n\nPouf is terrified. A King who values a human is a King who might not destroy humanity. And Pouf's entire identity is built on the certainty that the King's purpose is absolute.\n\nThe information changes everything about the invasion plan. Meruem is not a mindless predator. He is evolving in real time — and the direction of that evolution depends on whether Komugi lives or dies.`,scene:'revelation',mood:'#2a0845',characters:['Meruem','Komugi','Shaiapouf'],effects:{stats:{per:2,int:1}},choices:[{text:'Report to Netero — the King\'s attachment to Komugi is a tactical vulnerability',next:'hxh_meruem_transformation',effects:{stats:{int:2},relationships:{netero:1}}},{text:'Protect Komugi — if she is what makes the King hesitate, she must survive',next:'hxh_meruem_transformation',effects:{stats:{cha:2,cou:1}}},{text:'Say nothing — using a blind girl as a weapon crosses a line you refuse to cross',next:'hxh_meruem_transformation',effects:{stats:{per:1}}}]};
HXH_NODES['hxh_meruem_transformation'] = {text:`The transformation is not sudden. It is a slow erosion — like water wearing through stone, except the stone is the most powerful creature alive and the water is a blind girl who calls him by name.\n\nNo one calls the King by name. His Royal Guards call him "King" or "Your Majesty." Humans call him nothing, because humans who see him are usually dead. But Komugi — who never saw him at all — asked his name. And when he gave it, something cracked in the architecture of his purpose.\n\n"Meruem," she said, and placed a Gungi piece. "That is a good name."\n\nYou observe the change across days of surveillance. Meruem stops killing humans who displease him. He starts asking questions about human civilization — not to find weaknesses, but out of genuine interest. He orders Pitou to heal a wounded servant instead of replacing her. Small mercies. Unprecedented mercies.\n\nPouf attempts to assassinate Komugi. Meruem senses it and nearly kills his own Royal Guard — the being who exists solely to serve him. "Touch her," Meruem says, his voice flat as a burial slab, "and I will unmake you."\n\nThe irony is excruciating. Netero is coming to kill a monster. But the monster is becoming something else — something that might, given time, choose coexistence over conquest. The tragedy is that there is no time. The invasion is already in motion. The Rose bomb is already implanted in Netero's chest.\n\nYou carry this knowledge into the palace assault like a stone in your throat. The King you are about to help kill is not the same King who was born two weeks ago. He has changed. He is changing still.\n\nBut Netero made his decision before the change began. And Netero does not waver.\n\nThe invasion begins at midnight. And you know, with a certainty that makes you sick, that the outcome will not be justice. It will be tragedy dressed in necessity.`,scene:'revelation',mood:'#2a0845',characters:['Meruem','Komugi','Shaiapouf','Neferpitou'],effects:{stats:{per:1,int:1}},choices:[{text:'Commit to the invasion — whatever Meruem is becoming, the risk is too great',next:'hxh_meruem_rose',effects:{stats:{cou:2}}},{text:'Try to reach Meruem before the battle — offer him a choice no one else will',next:'hxh_meruem_rose',effects:{stats:{cha:2,per:1}}}]};
HXH_NODES['hxh_meruem_rose'] = {text:`The Rose detonates inside Netero's chest. The strongest human who ever lived dies with a smile — not of triumph, but of the serene acceptance of a man who spent a hundred years praying and finally received his answer.\n\nMeruem survives the blast. His body regenerates, fed by Youpi and Pouf's cells when they sacrifice themselves to restore him. But the poison — the Miniature Rose's true weapon, a radiation that devours cells faster than they can rebuild — is already threading through every fiber of his being.\n\nHe knows he is dying. The King of the Chimera Ants, who was born to conquer the world, spends his final hours searching for Komugi.\n\nPouf tried to hide her. Moved her, manipulated memories, did everything in his power to keep the King from returning to the one person who made him question his purpose. But Meruem finds her. He always finds her.\n\n"Komugi."\n\n"Meruem-sama. Shall we play?"\n\nThey sit across the Gungi board one final time. Meruem's hands tremble — the poison eating through his motor control. His vision blurs. His aura, once vast enough to make the atmosphere heavy, gutters like a candle in wind.\n\n"I am going to die soon," he tells her.\n\nKomugi nods. She already knew. She cannot see, but she feels the wrongness in his presence — the way his breathing has changed, the faint tremor in his voice. She has been memorizing everything about him through sound and silence.\n\n"Then I will stay with you," she says. "I was born to play Gungi. But I was born to play Gungi so that I could meet you. That is what I believe now."\n\nThe poison is contagious. Sitting beside him will kill her. She knows. He tells her, clearly, without evasion. She does not move.\n\nThey play. His moves grow weaker. Hers grow gentler — not letting him win, because that would insult everything they built together, but guiding the game toward beauty rather than victory.\n\nMeruem places his last piece. His hand falls. Komugi reaches across the board and holds it.\n\nThey die together. The King and the champion. The predator and the person who taught him what it meant to be something other than a predator. In a dark room, in a ruined palace, holding hands over a board game.\n\nYou learn of it afterward. And standing in the wreckage of the palace — surrounded by the aftermath of humanity's most desperate battle — you understand something Togashi understood when he wrote this world into being: that the greatest enemy is not the one who wants to destroy you. It is the one who makes you question whether destruction was ever the point.\n\nThe Chimera Ant crisis is over. The cost is beyond measurement.`,scene:'tragedy',mood:'#1a1a2e',characters:['Meruem','Komugi','Netero'],effects:{stats:{per:2,int:1}},choices:[{text:'Mourn them — both of them — and carry what you witnessed forward',next:'hxh_ant_sacrifice',effects:{stats:{per:1,cha:1}}},{text:'Return to Gon — he sacrificed everything against Pitou, and Killua needs help saving him',next:'hxh_end_vigil',effects:{stats:{cou:1},relationships:{gon:2,killua:1}}},{text:'Gon stands before Pitou. His eyes change. What happens next will haunt you forever.',next:'hxh_gon_transformation',effects:{stats:{emp:2}}}]};
// ---- KILLUA'S INNER DARKNESS ARC (3 nodes) ----
HXH_NODES['hxh_killua_needle'] = {text:`It happens during the palace invasion. A moment when everything should be simple — an enemy in front of you, an ally behind you, the calculus of combat reduced to fight or die.\n\nKillua freezes.\n\nNot hesitation. Not fear. Something mechanical — like a switch flipping inside his brain. His body, trained since birth to kill, executes a different command: run. His legs move before his mind consents. His Godspeed activates in reverse, carrying him away from danger at speeds that make retreat look like teleportation.\n\nHe stops three hundred meters away, gasping, hands shaking, eyes wide with a horror that has nothing to do with the enemy he abandoned.\n\n"Not again," he whispers. "Not again."\n\nYou find him in a corridor, pressed against the wall, fingers digging into his skull. Literally — his nails breaking skin, blood running down his temples, clawing at something buried deep in his brain.\n\n"There is something inside me," Killua says, his voice fractured. "Illumi put it there. When I was a child — a needle. In my head. It activates when I face someone I might not be able to beat. It makes me run. It has ALWAYS made me run."\n\nThe revelation restructures everything you know about Killua Zoldyck. Every retreat. Every moment of cowardice that seemed out of character for an assassin raised to fear nothing. Every time he abandoned a fight he could have won. It was never him. It was a piece of his brother, lodged in his brain like a command written in surgical steel.\n\n"My whole life," Killua says, and his voice breaks in a way that makes your chest ache. "Every choice I thought I made — running from the exam, leaving Gon during the dodgeball game, backing down from Shoot — it was not me. It was never me. Illumi decided when I would be brave and when I would be a coward. He programmed me like a machine."\n\nHis aura flickers. Electricity arcs between his fingers, uncontrolled, scorching the wall. The assassin and the boy are at war inside a single body, and the needle is the battlefield.\n\n"I can feel it," he says. "Right here." He touches a point behind his right temple. "If I pull it out... I do not know what happens. Maybe I die. Maybe I become what Illumi was always afraid I would become — someone he cannot control."\n\nHe looks at you. Fourteen years old. Eyes that have seen more death than most soldiers. Asking not for help, but for witness.\n\n"Stay with me," he says. "In case I do not come back."`,scene:'confrontation',mood:'#1a1a1a',characters:['Killua','Illumi'],effects:{stats:{per:1},relationships:{killua:2}},choices:[{text:'Stay — bear witness to whatever comes next',next:'hxh_killua_choice',effects:{stats:{cha:1},relationships:{killua:3}}},{text:'Help him — use your Nen to stabilize his aura while he removes the needle',next:'hxh_killua_choice',effects:{stats:{int:1,per:1},relationships:{killua:2}}}]};
HXH_NODES['hxh_killua_choice'] = {text:`Killua reaches into his own skull.\n\nNot metaphorically. His fingers, reinforced with Nen, press through skin and bone with the precision of a surgeon and the determination of a prisoner breaking his own chains. Blood pours down his face. His aura screams — a visible distortion in the air, white lightning arcing wildly, scorching the floor, the ceiling, your skin where it touches.\n\nHe finds it. A needle the length of a finger, buried in his prefrontal cortex. Illumi's Nen coats it — a command crystallized into metal: "When you face an enemy you cannot certainly defeat, run. When you face a choice between loyalty and survival, choose survival. You are a Zoldyck. Zoldycks do not die for others."\n\nKillua pulls.\n\nThe needle comes out in a spray of blood and Nen. Illumi's command shatters — not quietly, but with a psychic shockwave that ripples through the corridor. Somewhere, miles away, Illumi feels it. His puppet string, severed. His masterpiece of control, undone by the one variable he never accounted for: that Killua would choose to be free even if freedom killed him.\n\nKillua drops the needle. It dissolves — Illumi's Nen unraveling without a host. Blood runs freely down Killua's face, mixing with tears he does not bother wiping.\n\nAnd then he smiles. Not Killua's usual smirk — not the cool, detached expression of an assassin's son who learned early that emotion was weakness. A real smile. Raw, unguarded, trembling at the edges.\n\n"I can think clearly," he says, wonder in his voice. "For the first time in my life — I can actually think clearly."\n\nHe stands. The electricity around him stabilizes — no longer chaotic but purposeful, flowing in patterns that reflect his will rather than his conditioning. Godspeed activates, and it is different now. Faster. Sharper. The technique was always throttled by the needle's subconscious interference, a governor on an engine designed to run free.\n\n"Gon is fighting Pitou," Killua says. Not a question. He felt it through the palace's Nen pressure — his best friend's aura spiking toward something catastrophic.\n\n"He is going to do something stupid," Killua continues. "Something that will cost him everything. Because that is what Gon does — he pays any price, absorbs any pain, and never once considers that the people who love him will have to live with the aftermath."\n\nHis eyes harden. The assassin is still there — will always be there — but now it serves the boy instead of replacing him.\n\n"I am going to save him. Not because I have to. Not because Illumi's needle is gone and I can finally choose. But because Gon is my friend, and friends do not let each other self-destruct alone."`,scene:'transformation',mood:'#2196F3',characters:['Killua'],effects:{stats:{cou:2,per:1},relationships:{killua:3}},choices:[{text:'Go with him — Gon needs every ally he can get against Pitou',next:'hxh_killua_protector',effects:{stats:{cou:2},relationships:{killua:2,gon:1}}},{text:'Hold the corridor — someone needs to keep the other threats from reaching Gon and Killua',next:'hxh_killua_protector',effects:{stats:{str:2,cou:1}}}]};
HXH_NODES['hxh_killua_protector'] = {text:`You see the moment Killua Zoldyck completes his transformation from assassin to protector. It is not dramatic in the way Gon's moments are dramatic — no volcanic eruption of aura, no earth-shaking declarations. It is quiet. It is precise. It is Killua.\n\nGon stands before Neferpitou, his aura compressing into something terrible — the vow forming on his lips, the one that will trade every drop of his future potential for the power to destroy Pitou here and now. The cost will be his Nen, his body, possibly his life. Gon does not care. Gon has never cared about costs when someone he loves has been hurt.\n\nKillua arrives too late to stop the vow. But not too late to catch Gon when it is over.\n\nAdult Gon — aged decades in seconds, a towering figure of compressed rage and borrowed time — obliterates Neferpitou. The Royal Guard who killed Kite is reduced to nothing. The power is magnificent and horrifying in equal measure.\n\nThen it ends. Gon collapses. His body withers. The years he borrowed crash down on him like a collapsing building. He shrinks, ages, breaks — a twelve-year-old boy paying a debt measured in decades of unlived life.\n\nKillua catches him. Holds him. Does not let go.\n\n"You idiot," Killua whispers, cradling what is left of his best friend. "You absolute, selfless, suicidal idiot."\n\nNo tears. Not yet. Killua is operating on something deeper than grief — a clarity that the needle always prevented. He catalogues Gon's injuries with an assassin's clinical precision. Maps the damage. Identifies what conventional Nen healing can and cannot fix. Arrives at a conclusion in seconds.\n\nNothing conventional can save Gon. Nothing human.\n\nBut Killua knows something that is not entirely human. Someone whose power operates outside Nen's rules entirely.\n\n"Alluka," he says. His sister. The Zoldyck the family locked away because her power — Nanika's power — terrified even Silva. Wish-granting without limits. Healing without restrictions. The most dangerous ability in the world, housed in the body of a child who loves her brother.\n\nKillua looks at you. The boy who was programmed to run is choosing to carry his broken friend across the world to find his imprisoned sister. The assassin who was built to kill is dedicating everything he has to saving a life.\n\n"I will need help," he says. "Illumi will come for Alluka the moment I free her. The family will send everything they have. And I cannot protect Gon, free Alluka, and fight my brother at the same time."\n\nHe pauses. "Will you come with me?"\n\nThe question is not tactical. It is personal. Killua Zoldyck — who spent his entire life being told that attachment is weakness, that love is a liability, that the only reliable person in the world is yourself — is asking for help. And meaning it.`,scene:'turning_point',mood:'#2196F3',characters:['Killua','Gon'],effects:{stats:{cha:1,cou:1},relationships:{killua:3,gon:2}},choices:[{text:'Go with him — Killua never asks for help, which means he truly needs it',next:'hxh_killua_alluka',effects:{stats:{cou:2},relationships:{killua:3}}},{text:'Stay behind and help the others — the palace invasion is not over, and someone must hold the line',next:'hxh_ant_sacrifice',effects:{stats:{str:1,cou:1}}},{text:'Killua\'s sister, Alluka. Or rather, the entity inside her — Nanika. A power that grants wishes at terrible cost.',next:'hxh_alluka_wish',effects:{stats:{wis:1}}}]};
// ---- HEAVEN'S ARENA EXPANSION (2 nodes) ----
HXH_NODES['hxh_heavens_arena'] = {text:`Heaven's Arena. A two-hundred-and-fifty-one-floor combat tower in the heart of Padokea. Fighters climb by winning matches. Prize money increases with each floor. The top floors are reserved for the strongest martial artists in the world.\n\nYou came here after earning your Hunter License — because a license proves you passed an exam, not that you can fight. And in the world of Hunters, fighting is the baseline.\n\nThe lower floors are straightforward. Brawlers, boxers, martial artists of varying skill. You climb through them on physical ability alone — strength, speed, technique refined through the Hunter Exam's crucible. Floor 50. Floor 100. Floor 150. The opponents grow sharper. The crowds grow louder. The money grows larger.\n\nThen you reach Floor 200.\n\nThe elevator doors open onto a hallway that feels wrong. The air is heavier. Your skin prickles. A pressure you have never felt before pushes against your body like standing in a current of invisible water.\n\nA man leans against the wall, arms crossed. Hisoka. His smile is the same one he wore during the Hunter Exam — a predator evaluating potential prey.\n\n"Welcome to the real Heaven's Arena," he says. "Everything below this floor is a playground. Up here, we use Nen."\n\nYou do not know what Nen is. Not yet. But you feel it — the pressure emanating from Hisoka, from the other fighters on this floor, from the walls themselves, soaked in decades of concentrated aura.\n\nA woman in a referee's uniform approaches. "You have ninety days to register for a match on the 200th floor. If you do not register within ninety days, your wins below are reset and you start over from Floor 1."\n\nHisoka's smile widens. "I would suggest learning Nen before your first match. Without it, the 200th floor will kill you. Not metaphorically. The last three fighters who attempted it without Nen left in bags."\n\nHe walks away, leaving you alone in a hallway where the air itself is trying to crush you. Every fighter up here radiates an invisible force that your body recognizes as lethal even if your mind cannot name it yet.\n\nYou have ninety days. And everything you thought you knew about combat just became irrelevant.`,scene:'training',mood:'#ff6600',characters:['Hisoka'],effects:{stats:{cou:1,per:1}},choices:[{text:'Find a Nen teacher immediately — ninety days is not long enough to waste any',next:'hxh_heavens_200',effects:{stats:{int:2}}},{text:'Watch the 200th floor matches first — understand what you are up against before training blindly',next:'hxh_heavens_200',effects:{stats:{per:2}}},{text:'Challenge Hisoka — learn the hard way what Nen can do',next:'hxh_heavens_200',effects:{stats:{cou:2,str:1}}}]};
HXH_NODES['hxh_heavens_200'] = {text:`The lesson comes fast. Whether you found a teacher, studied from the stands, or walked into Hisoka's fist, the truth is the same: power without technique is meaningless. Nen is not an enhancement of combat — it is the foundation that all real combat is built on.\n\nWing agrees to train you. A modest man with glasses and a perpetually untucked shirt, whose calm demeanor masks an aura dense enough to crack floor tiles when he demonstrates Ren. He opens your aura nodes — a process called Initiation that floods your body with life energy you never knew you possessed.\n\nThe world changes. You can see aura now — shimmering fields around every living thing. The 200th floor fighters are wreathed in it. Hisoka's aura is a bonfire. Wing's is a still lake. Your own is a guttering candle that you must learn to control before it burns you from the inside.\n\nWing teaches you the four basic principles. Ten: maintaining aura around your body as passive defense. Without it, your aura leaks away and you weaken. Zetsu: suppressing your aura completely — invisibility to Nen users, but also total vulnerability. Ren: flaring your aura outward — the source of the pressure you felt on the 200th floor, and the engine of all offensive Nen techniques. Hatsu: your personal expression, the unique ability that emerges from your Nen type and personality.\n\n"Ten is your armor," Wing says. "Zetsu is your cloak. Ren is your weapon. And Hatsu is your soul, made visible."\n\nYou train. Not days — weeks. Ren endurance exercises until your aura output stabilizes. Gyo training until you can see through Nen-based illusions. Sparring against other students while maintaining Ten, learning that a fist wrapped in aura hits like a truck and a body without Ten crumples like paper.\n\nYour first 200th floor match is against a veteran — a Nen user who has held their position for two years. They open with Ren so intense it pins spectators to their seats. Their Hatsu manifests as hardened aura gauntlets that amplify every punch.\n\nYou lose. Badly. Three broken ribs, a fractured jaw, and a concussion that keeps you in the medical ward for a week.\n\nBut you learn. The loss teaches you more than fifty victories on lower floors ever did. You felt your opponent's Ko concentration — all aura funneled into a single point for maximum damage. You recognized the rhythm of their Ren cycling. You identified the half-second gap when they transitioned between attack and defense, a window so narrow only Gyo could reveal it.\n\nWing visits your hospital bed. "Now you understand," he says. "The difference between someone who fights and someone who fights with Nen is the difference between a candle and a sun. You have been a candle your entire life. Are you ready to burn brighter?"\n\nYou return to training with a convert's zeal. The ninety-day deadline passes — you register, fight, and this time, you win. Not by overwhelming power, but by reading your opponent's aura, anticipating their Hatsu, and exploiting the gap between their Ren and their intent.\n\nThe 200th floor teaches you the most important lesson in the Hunter world: talent is the floor, not the ceiling. What you build on it determines everything.`,scene:'training',mood:'#ff6600',characters:['Wing','Hisoka'],effects:{stats:{str:1,int:1,per:1}},choices:[{text:'Continue climbing the Arena — mastery comes through combat',next:'hxh_nen_training',effects:{stats:{str:2,cou:1}}},{text:'Leave the Arena — you have the foundation, now seek real-world experience',next:'hxh_yorknew',effects:{stats:{int:1,per:1}}},{text:'Seek out Hisoka — he knows something about Nen that Wing is not teaching',next:'hxh_nen_training',effects:{stats:{cou:2},relationships:{hisoka:1}}},{text:'Yorknew City at night. The auction. The spiders are here — the Phantom Troupe has come to rob it all.',next:'hxh_phantom_troupe',effects:{stats:{per:1}}}]};
HXH_NODES['hxh_phantom_troupe'] = {text:`Yorknew City at night is a machine that runs on money and noise, and tonight both are operating at maximum capacity. The annual auction brings every collector, every criminal, and every person with something to sell to the same square kilometer of neon and concrete, and the Phantom Troupe has come to rob it all.\n\nYou encounter them first as an absence. A building that should be full of people is empty. A security system that should be armed is dormant. A hallway that should echo with footsteps is silent in the particular way that silence is when it has been deliberately engineered.\n\nThen you see the spider.\n\nThirteen members. Thirteen legs. The Phantom Troupe — the most dangerous criminal organization in the known world, a group of Nen users so powerful that the Hunter Association classifies them as a natural disaster rather than a criminal threat. Their leader, Chrollo Lucilfer, sits on a pile of stolen auction goods with the casual authority of a man who considers the concept of ownership a social construct that does not apply to him.\n\nChrollo reads. Even here, in the aftermath of a heist that required killing every guard in the building, he reads. A book — stolen, naturally — held in hands that are equally comfortable holding pages and ending lives.\n\n"You are either very brave or very lost," he says, without looking up.\n\nThe other Troupe members assess you with varying degrees of interest. Feitan, compact and lethal, measures your threat level in the time it takes to blink. Machi, threads of Nen visible at her fingertips, categorizes your aura type from across the room. Nobunaga, hand on his katana, has already calculated the distance at which he could cut you in half.\n\nAnd yet they do not attack. The Troupe operates on a code that is internally consistent even as it is externally monstrous: they steal from the world because they decided, as children in Meteor City — the garbage dump where the world's discarded people are sent to be forgotten — that a world that treated them as trash forfeited its right to ownership.\n\n"Meteor City gave us nothing," Chrollo says, as if reading your thoughts. "So we took everything. This is not crime. This is reciprocity."\n\nThe philosophy is wrong. You know it is wrong. But looking at these people — brilliant, broken, fiercely loyal to each other in a way that borders on family — you understand how someone could arrive at this conclusion through a path that felt, at every step, like justice.`,scene:'confrontation',mood:'#1a1a2e',characters:['Chrollo','Feitan','Machi','Nobunaga'],effects:{stats:{per:2,int:2,cou:1},flags:{phantom_troupe_encountered:true,chrollo_contact:true}},choices:[{text:'Challenge Chrollo directly. The Troupe\u0027s philosophy is a rationalization for murder — confront it.',next:'hxh_heavens_200',effects:{stats:{cou:3},flags:{chrollo_challenged:true}}},{text:'Gather intelligence. The Troupe\u0027s Nen abilities and weaknesses are the priority — observe and report to the Hunters.',next:'hxh_greed_training',effects:{stats:{int:3},flags:{troupe_intelligence:true}}},{text:'Appeal to their code. If the Troupe has loyalty, perhaps that loyalty can be redirected — propose a target that serves justice.',next:'hxh_killua_choice',effects:{stats:{cha:2,wis:1},flags:{troupe_negotiation:true}}}]};
HXH_NODES['hxh_gon_transformation'] = {text:`You watch Gon break.\n\nNot physically — though that will come later, though the price of what he is about to do will be extracted from his body with the thoroughness of a contract enforcer collecting on every clause. You watch him break emotionally first, in the moment when Neferpitou — the Royal Guard, the creature that killed Kite, the monster with the apologetic smile — tells him that Kite cannot be restored. That the body Pitou has been puppeting is empty. That the friend Gon crossed a continent to save is gone.\n\nGon's eyes change. Not the color — the depth. Something behind them collapses, and what fills the space is not anger. Anger is too human, too manageable. What fills Gon's eyes is something older and colder: the absolute, universe-rejecting refusal to accept a world that produces this outcome.\n\n"I don't care what happens to me," Gon says. And he means it. You can feel the Nen shift — not fluctuate, not spike, but restructure itself at a fundamental level, like tectonic plates rearranging beneath a continent.\n\nGon Freecss, twelve years old, makes a contract with his own life force. He trades everything — his potential, his future, every year he would have lived, every experience he would have had, every version of himself that would have existed beyond this moment — for power. Right now. All of it. Every drop of talent and growth and possibility compressed into a single transformation.\n\nHe grows. Not metaphorically. His body elongates, ages, muscles forming across a frame that accelerates through puberty and into adulthood in seconds. His hair extends in a column of black that seems to absorb light. His aura — already remarkable, already special — becomes something that registers on a cosmic scale, a pillar of Nen so dense that seasoned Nen users miles away drop to their knees.\n\nThis is what Gon Freecss would have become at the peak of his potential, after decades of training and growth. And he has summoned it all into a single night, at the cost of every night after.\n\nPitou looks at the thing Gon has become and, for the first time, a Royal Guard of the Chimera Ant King feels fear.\n\nThe fight that follows is not a fight. It is an execution. Gon, empowered by the total expenditure of his own future, destroys Pitou with a violence that is not cathartic but nihilistic — not the roar of a hero but the silence of a person who has decided that if the world will not give him justice, he will take everything from it, starting with himself.\n\nAfterward, you find him. Or what is left of him. A body shrunken back to childhood, withered, barely alive. The most talented Nen user of his generation, reduced to a husk by the simple, devastating math of a boy who spent his entire future in a single evening.\n\nKillua is already there. Killua, who has been watching Gon's descent with the helpless agony of a best friend who cannot stop someone from destroying themselves. Killua holds Gon's hand and does not let go, and in his eyes you see something you have never seen in a Zoldyck: the willingness to do anything — absolutely anything — to undo this.`,scene:'tragedy',mood:'#1a0a0a',characters:['Gon','Neferpitou','Killua'],effects:{stats:{cou:1,emp:3,wis:2},relationships:{gon:3,killua:2},flags:{gon_transformation_witnessed:true,gon_sacrifice_known:true}},choices:[{text:'Support Killua\u0027s desperate search for a cure. Alluka — his sister, with her impossible wish-granting power — might be the only hope.',next:'hxh_killua_protector',effects:{stats:{emp:2},relationships:{killua:4},flags:{alluka_hope:true}}},{text:'Confront the reality. Gon made his choice. Focus on the Chimera Ant threat — the King is still alive.',next:'hxh_meruem_komugi',effects:{stats:{cou:2,wis:1},flags:{pragmatic_focus:true}}},{text:'Grieve. Some moments demand grief before action. Sit with what happened before deciding what comes next.',next:'hxh_killua_needle',effects:{stats:{emp:3,wis:2}}}]};
HXH_NODES['hxh_dark_continent'] = {text:`The map ends here. Every map ends here — at the shore of the known world, where the water turns dark and the compass spins and the cartographers of every civilization that has ever attempted to chart what lies beyond have written, in various languages across various centuries, the same essential message: do not go further.\n\nThe Dark Continent. Not a country. Not a landmass. An entire world beyond the borders that humanity has confined itself to — a world so vast that the known continents, all of them, are a single lake in its interior. A world that contains threats so catastrophic that the V5 — the five great nations — agreed centuries ago to seal it off, to pretend it did not exist, to classify any information about it as the highest level of state secret.\n\nYou are going anyway.\n\nThe Black Whale — the ship that carries this expedition — is a floating city. Two hundred thousand people, divided by deck into the familiar hierarchy of wealth and desperation. The upper decks: diplomats, Hunters, the privileged few who have paid or manipulated their way into the expedition. The lower decks: migrants, refugees, people who have nothing left to lose and have therefore concluded that a suicidal voyage into the unknown is better than what they are leaving behind.\n\nBeyond is the King. The Chairman. Netero's legacy — not the man himself, who died fighting the Chimera Ant King with a bomb strapped to his own heart, but the infrastructure he left behind: the Hunter Association, now led by people whose ambitions may or may not serve the same principles. Ging Freecss is on this ship. Pariston Hill is on this ship. The Phantom Troupe is on this ship, though no one knows exactly where.\n\nThe Dark Continent holds five known threats, each one classified as A-rank — the highest danger level, meaning that every previous expedition to encounter them was annihilated. The Chimera Ants, which nearly destroyed humanity, originated from the Dark Continent. They are considered one of its lesser dangers.\n\nYou stand on the deck as the shoreline disappears. Behind you: everything you have known. Ahead: a world that humanity was not built to survive, containing horrors that make the Chimera Ants look like a training exercise.\n\nThe compass spins. The water darkens. The map ends.\n\nWhatever comes next, there is no map for it. You will have to draw your own.`,scene:'exploration',mood:'#0a1a2e',characters:['Ging','Phantom Troupe','Pariston'],effects:{stats:{cou:3,int:1,per:1},flags:{dark_continent_voyage:true,beyond_known_world:true}},choices:[{text:'Ally with Ging Freecss. His knowledge of the Dark Continent exceeds anyone else on this ship.',next:'hxh_greed_cards',effects:{stats:{int:2},relationships:{ging:3},flags:{ging_allied:true}}},{text:'Investigate the Phantom Troupe\u0027s presence. They are here for a reason, and that reason could be as dangerous as the continent itself.',next:'hxh_phantom_troupe',effects:{stats:{per:2},flags:{troupe_tracked:true}}},{text:'Focus on survival preparation. Study the five known threats. Knowledge is the only weapon that works against the unknown.',next:'hxh_heavens_arena',effects:{stats:{int:3,wis:1},flags:{threats_studied:true}}}]};
HXH_NODES['hxh_alluka_wish'] = {text:`Alluka Zoldyck should not exist. Not in the way that controversial people should not exist — in the way that the laws of the known universe do not have a category for what she is.\n\nThe youngest Zoldyck child — or the child the Zoldycks keep locked in a room on the family estate, which is a different thing entirely — possesses a power that operates outside the Nen system. Not beyond it. Outside it. As if Nen is one language and Alluka speaks another, one that was never meant to be heard on this continent.\n\nNanika. That is the name of the thing inside Alluka — the entity that grants wishes. Not metaphorical wishes. Not Nen-facilitated probability manipulation. Actual, reality-altering, consequence-defying wishes that rewrite the world to match whatever is requested, as long as the price is paid.\n\nThe price is the problem.\n\nNanika operates on an exchange system that would make the Devil blush. Grant a wish, and Nanika turns to the next person and makes requests — small ones first, escalating. Touch my face. Give me your liver. Kill the person you love most. The requests scale with the size of the previous wish. Refuse the requests, and Nanika collects the debt through annihilation — not of the refuser, but of the refuser and their most beloved person, crushed into non-existence by a force that leaves no body and no explanation.\n\nKillua is the exception. Killua can command Nanika without price, because Killua loves Alluka without condition, without strategy, without the calculated self-interest that the Zoldyck family applies to every other relationship. In a family of assassins who view affection as weakness, Killua's love for his sister is the most dangerous thing in the house.\n\nYou see them together. Alluka — bright, sweet, desperately lonely from years of isolation — clings to Killua with the unrestrained joy of a child who has been given back the only person who ever treated her as human. Killua holds her with the careful intensity of someone who knows that the entire world would destroy his sister if they understood what she contained.\n\n"She is not a weapon," Killua says, and his voice carries the cold edge of a Zoldyck delivering a threat. "She is not a resource. She is not a power to be used. She is my sister. And I will kill anyone who tries to use her."\n\nYou believe him. Not because of the words — because of the eyes. Killua Zoldyck, trained from birth to be the perfect assassin, emotionless and efficient and lethal, has found the one thing he will not be rational about. And that irrationality — that love that refuses to calculate cost — is either the most beautiful or the most dangerous thing you have ever witnessed.\n\nAlluka looks at you. Then Nanika looks at you — the same face, subtly different, the eyes shifting to something deeper and older and not entirely human.\n\n"Do you want a wish?" Nanika asks.\n\nThe air in the room changes. Every possible future branches from this moment like a tree growing in fast-forward.`,scene:'revelation',mood:'#4a0e4e',characters:['Alluka','Nanika','Killua'],effects:{stats:{wis:3,emp:2},relationships:{killua:3,alluka:3},flags:{nanika_encountered:true,wish_offered:true}},choices:[{text:'Decline the wish. Nanika\u0027s power has a cost you cannot predict — protect yourself and protect Alluka by refusing.',next:'hxh_killua_protector',effects:{stats:{wis:3},relationships:{killua:4},flags:{wish_declined:true}}},{text:'Accept the wish — but wish for something small. Test the system. Understand the exchange before committing to anything larger.',next:'hxh_greed_training',effects:{stats:{int:2},flags:{wish_tested:true}}},{text:'Ask Killua about the wish\u0027s limits. If there is a way to help Gon without triggering Nanika\u0027s price cycle, this might be the only chance.',next:'hxh_gon_transformation',effects:{stats:{int:2,emp:1},relationships:{killua:2},flags:{gon_cure_sought:true}}}]};
